{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And the earth was tohu.</b> The earth, now built up, had already been tohu. When it was created at the beginning, it was tohu and vohu — that is, desolate and empty, having in it neither grass, man, beast, or domestic animal, bird, fish, or creeping creature, darkness, light, or wind — as the Jerusalem Targum renders: וארעא הות צדיא מבני אנשא וריקניא מן בעירא — for waters were upon the face of all the earth." "<b>Tohu.</b> As in ויתעם בתהו, and ראיתי את הארץ והנה תהו. One should not interpret that before its creation it was tohu and vohu, for if so, it should have written והארץ היה תהו ובהו — meaning, what is now earth had previously been tohu and vohu — but since it says <b>היתה</b>, it refers back to the earth. Another interpretation: והארץ היתה תהו ובהו — the verse speaks of the future; meaning, before HaShem created the heavens and the earth, that place where the earth was destined to stand was tohu and vohu. And likewise <b>על פני תהום</b> — over the face of the place where the deep was destined to stand. And likewise <b>מרחפת על פני המים</b> — for these matters had not yet been created. And there are many matters whose creation is not publicized here, such as angels, the divine chariot, and Gehenna; rather, only things that we see in the world. And even ויטע גן בעדן was written only because of Man's sin, that he was banished from there. But the verse encompasses their creation all at once, as it is written ויכולו השמים והארץ וכל צבאם, and it says אתה עשית את השמים שמי השמים וכל צבאם. And it says כי ששת ימים עשה ה׳ את השמים ואת הארץ את הים ואת כל אשר בם — meaning the seas and all that is in them. And do not wonder about the word <b>היתה</b>, which is feminine, when it speaks of a masculine matter, for we find its parallel: ויראו את העם אשר בקרבה יושבת לבטח, and ארון האלקים נלקחה, and אם ככה את עושה לי, and ותכל דוד המלך. The vav of <b>והארץ</b> is superfluous, like the vav of ולא נחם אלקים, and many like it." "<b>And the earth was tohu and vohu.</b> This expression is not said of the heavens, only because of the honor of the Shekhinah that dwells in them. But according to the Talmud's reasoning, it implies that they too were created as tohu and vohu, and one must explain: וסביבות קצות הארץ ברא תהו ובהו." "<b>And darkness upon the face of the deep.</b> It refers back to ברא: HaShem created <b>darkness upon the face of the deep</b> — that darkness is a created thing, as it is written יוצר אור ובורא חשך. But beforehand there was no darkness or light, and no eye saw — apart from HaShem — and one must not expound or speculate about what preceded it, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said: one must not expound or ask what was before or what was after." "<b>And the spirit of Elohim.</b> It refers back to ברא. Likewise, interpret בראשית ברא: HaShem created heavens and earth and darkness, and the Holy Spirit that was hovering upon the face of the waters." "<b>And wind.</b> Four winds blow every day. From morning until midday, an easterly wind, and it is hot and moist; from then until evening, a southerly wind, and it is hot and dry; from then until midnight, a westerly wind, and it is cold and moist; from then until morning, a northerly wind, cold and dry." "<b>And the wind of Elohim.</b> That wind was needed to gather the waters, as it is written 'יקוו המים' — as you say, 'YHWH drove the sea with a strong east wind' etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {1 "In the series of examples, each illustrates a feminine verb or participle attached to a masculine noun; the fourth example ותכל דוד המלך follows the same pattern."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:18:28.343538328Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}